<p>For years, I&rsquo;ve been using verbatim <a href="https://orgmode.org/manual/Emphasis-and-Monospace.html">emphasis</a> in org-mode to mark files and keyboard strokes, while reserving code emphasis to show function names, variables, and other elements of code.</p>
<p>With the reintroduction of <code>&lt;kbd&gt;&lt;/kbd&gt;</code> tags to the blog <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/07/26/emacs-config-gems-part.html">yesterday</a><sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup>, I wanted these tags to follow the same logic derived from my usage in org-mode: if I used verbatim in org-mode to indicate keystrokes and filenames, then I should use <code>&lt;kbd&gt;&lt;/kbd&gt;</code> tags on the blog to indicate the same thing. I explained in a footnote in the post: &ldquo;The reason I have it different here is because I often talk about files in my posts without referring to code.&rdquo; That might have been my preference, but it was wrong. We&rsquo;ll get back to it in a minute.</p>
<p>I wanted to get my markdown-exported posts to preserve the verbatim emphasis from org-mode. But Markdown doesn&rsquo;t distinguish between verbatim and code emphasis in org-mode — it treats both as <code>``</code>, or <code>&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;</code> tags in HTML. So I started looking for a way to fix this.</p>
<p>The abridged version (the research took over an hour): I ended up with a wrapper function that would add Markdown raw code injection specifically for that code (I&rsquo;m still working on it) to work with Pandoc. Why Pandoc and not Emacs&rsquo;s built-in Markdown exporter? Because Emacs&rsquo;s built-in exporter outputs certain characters, like my now <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/07/25/i-love-me-some-em.html">beloved em-dash again</a>, as character reference numbers, which results in a character reference (<code>&amp;#x2014</code> specifically) instead of an em-dash. Why&rsquo;s that? Look, have you eaten breakfast today? Because I haven&rsquo;t. Let me just tell you, these rabbits dig deep holes.</p>
<p>So now, after I had a working way (more or less) to produce my newly introduced <code>&lt;kbd&gt;&lt;/kbd&gt;</code> tags, I wanted to start using them for filenames. But I was bothered by the fact that while I can do whatever the hell I want this is not considered standard. And when I say &ldquo;bothered,&rdquo; I mean this is the thought I woke up to at around 05:40 this morning, which is what you get from a delirious dork Emacs brain like mine.</p>
<p>I knew HTML has standards, but I didn&rsquo;t connect the logic behind it to semantic HTML — not until I browsed on my phone, in my bed, for another half an hour of impulsive research. Turns out, this is something that some folks take <a href="https://validator.w3.org/nu/">very</a> <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org">seriously</a>. And according to these folks, filenames and paths do not and should not have a dedicated HTML tag (surely not <code>&lt;kbd&gt;&lt;/kbd&gt;</code>, which is for <del>keystrokes only</del> — actually, it&rsquo;s for &ldquo;textual user input&rdquo; which can include voice, for example). Authority has it that I should be indicating filenames using the <code>&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;</code> tags (and code emphasis in org-mode), and there&rsquo;s a good reason for that. Just take my word for it for now (breakfast, remember?)</p>
<p>Reading through their tomb of standards, I realized I need to correct my headings on the blog too (I jump directly to <code>&lt;h3&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;</code> tags instead of going by order, because the CSS for these tags looks better — in other words, I&rsquo;ve been lazy by not fixing this), among potentially many other things. There are over a <em>hundred</em> semantic tags in HTML, and there&rsquo;s a reason for each one.</p>
<p>Damn those rabbits.</p>
